1
00:00:00,594 --> 00:00:01,057
Sir.

2
00:00:01,097 --> 00:00:03,233
A new field report from planet Earth.

3
00:00:03,624 --> 00:00:04,465
Today's subject:

4
00:00:04,505 --> 00:00:05,566
cryptocurrency.

5
00:00:05,606 --> 00:00:07,448
Money the humans invented without a bank.

6
00:00:07,488 --> 00:00:08,435
And without paper.

7
00:00:08,475 --> 00:00:09,816
I will tell you what it is, where

8
00:00:09,856 --> 00:00:11,271
it came from, and who made it.

9
00:00:11,311 --> 00:00:12,614
And how one of them paid for two

10
00:00:12,654 --> 00:00:14,518
pizzas with what is now worth hundreds of

11
00:00:14,558 --> 00:00:15,574
millions of dollars.

12
00:00:15,614 --> 00:00:16,586
The year 2010.

13
00:00:16,626 --> 00:00:17,923
Ten thousand bitcoin...

14
00:00:17,963 --> 00:00:18,922
for two pizzas.

15
00:00:18,962 --> 00:00:20,372
At the prices of recent years, that comes

16
00:00:20,412 --> 00:00:21,874
to hundreds of millions of dollars.

17
00:00:21,914 --> 00:00:23,358
The most expensive dinner on the planet.

18
00:00:23,398 --> 00:00:24,439
And not even deep dish.

19
00:00:24,479 --> 00:00:26,200
But for Command to understand any of this,

20
00:00:26,240 --> 00:00:27,500
I have to start with the first thing

21
00:00:27,540 --> 00:00:28,423
I studied here:

22
00:00:28,463 --> 00:00:29,530
money itself.

23
00:00:29,580 --> 00:00:30,516
I checked their history.

24
00:00:30,556 --> 00:00:32,043
Long ago they swapped things.

25
00:00:32,083 --> 00:00:33,211
A fish for a loaf of bread.

26
00:00:33,251 --> 00:00:33,842
The problem:

27
00:00:33,882 --> 00:00:35,437
if the baker wants no fish today, you

28
00:00:35,477 --> 00:00:36,068
go hungry.

29
00:00:36,108 --> 00:00:36,813
Holding a fish.

30
00:00:36,853 --> 00:00:38,683
So they tried other things everybody would take.

31
00:00:38,723 --> 00:00:39,151
Salt.

32
00:00:39,191 --> 00:00:39,713
Shells.

33
00:00:39,753 --> 00:00:41,235
And on one island, stones as big as

34
00:00:41,275 --> 00:00:41,765
a car.

35
00:00:41,805 --> 00:00:43,029
Imagine asking for change.

36
00:00:43,069 --> 00:00:44,245
They settled on gold:

37
00:00:44,285 --> 00:00:46,683
rare, never rots, hard to fake.

38
00:00:47,379 --> 00:00:48,411
But gold is heavy.

39
00:00:48,451 --> 00:00:50,174
So they left it with someone trusted and

40
00:00:50,214 --> 00:00:50,745
took a receipt.

41
00:00:50,785 --> 00:00:52,468
The receipt started changing hands:

42
00:00:52,508 --> 00:00:53,155
paper money.

43
00:00:53,195 --> 00:00:54,739
Then the trusted man discovered he could lend

44
00:00:54,779 --> 00:00:55,817
your gold to someone else while you were

45
00:00:55,857 --> 00:00:56,411
not looking.

46
00:00:56,451 --> 00:00:57,678
And that, sir, is how the bank was

47
00:00:57,718 --> 00:00:58,044
born.

48
00:00:58,084 --> 00:01:00,120
In 1971 the dollar was cut loose from

49
00:01:00,160 --> 00:01:00,660
gold.

50
00:01:00,700 --> 00:01:01,988
The paper stood on its own.

51
00:01:02,028 --> 00:01:03,672
And whoever runs the printing press can print

52
00:01:03,712 --> 00:01:04,626
as much as they like.

53
00:01:04,666 --> 00:01:06,252
In Zimbabwe, they printed a single note for

54
00:01:06,292 --> 00:01:07,742
one hundred trillion dollars.

55
00:01:07,782 --> 00:01:09,248
It would not buy a bus ticket.

56
00:01:09,292 --> 00:01:11,265
So money's value comes from one thing:

57
00:01:11,305 --> 00:01:12,565
everyone believes in it.

58
00:01:12,605 --> 00:01:13,540
A group agreement.

59
00:01:13,580 --> 00:01:14,881
Like a line at the coffee shop.

60
00:01:14,921 --> 00:01:16,606
It works until somebody cuts.

61
00:01:16,646 --> 00:01:18,147
And today most of their money is not

62
00:01:18,187 --> 00:01:18,857
even paper.

63
00:01:18,897 --> 00:01:20,057
It is numbers on a screen.

64
00:01:20,097 --> 00:01:21,584
Most money on this planet has no physical

65
00:01:21,624 --> 00:01:21,958
form.

66
00:01:21,998 --> 00:01:22,872
Entries in tables.

67
00:01:22,912 --> 00:01:24,193
And they are perfectly comfortable with that.

68
00:01:24,233 --> 00:01:25,712
I also recorded that their money has three

69
00:01:25,752 --> 00:01:26,250
jobs.

70
00:01:26,290 --> 00:01:26,916
You pay with it.

71
00:01:26,956 --> 00:01:27,956
You price things with it.

72
00:01:27,996 --> 00:01:29,185
And you save it for tomorrow.

73
00:01:29,225 --> 00:01:30,285
Keep those three in the file, sir.

74
00:01:30,325 --> 00:01:31,271
We will need them later.

75
00:01:31,376 --> 00:01:33,161
I traced those numbers to where they actually

76
00:01:33,201 --> 00:01:33,687
live:

77
00:01:33,727 --> 00:01:34,584
in a ledger.

78
00:01:34,624 --> 00:01:36,451
Kept by an entity called the bank.

79
00:01:36,491 --> 00:01:39,470
When Alice pays Bob thirty dollars, nothing moves.

80
00:01:39,510 --> 00:01:41,650
The bank erases a number here, writes one

81
00:01:41,690 --> 00:01:42,464
there.

82
00:01:42,504 --> 00:01:43,297
Your salary:

83
00:01:43,337 --> 00:01:43,971
a number.

84
00:01:44,011 --> 00:01:44,605
Your rent:

85
00:01:44,645 --> 00:01:45,220
a number.

86
00:01:45,260 --> 00:01:47,253
You have never actually seen this money.

87
00:01:47,293 --> 00:01:48,905
Every bank keeps its own ledger.

88
00:01:48,945 --> 00:01:50,597
So every night they check each other's.

89
00:01:50,637 --> 00:01:52,276
Because none of them trusts the others.

90
00:01:52,316 --> 00:01:53,637
So a bank is not a vault full

91
00:01:53,677 --> 00:01:54,166
of money.

92
00:01:54,206 --> 00:01:55,461
A bank is a ledger.

93
00:01:55,501 --> 00:01:57,606
And whoever holds the ledger holds everything.

94
00:01:57,646 --> 00:01:59,942
I followed one transfer from start to finish.

95
00:01:59,982 --> 00:02:02,202
Every cent that moves between two humans has

96
00:02:02,242 --> 00:02:03,632
to pass through a third party.

97
00:02:03,672 --> 00:02:04,638
Alice sends.

98
00:02:04,678 --> 00:02:05,718
The bank checks.

99
00:02:05,758 --> 00:02:06,882
Then Bob receives it.

100
00:02:06,922 --> 00:02:07,853
Minus the fee.

101
00:02:07,938 --> 00:02:10,120
The ledger holder can charge a fee, delay

102
00:02:10,160 --> 00:02:12,762
you, freeze your account, or go bankrupt with

103
00:02:12,802 --> 00:02:13,702
your money.

104
00:02:13,743 --> 00:02:14,632
This is not a theory.

105
00:02:14,672 --> 00:02:16,905
On the island of Cyprus, in 2013, people

106
00:02:16,945 --> 00:02:18,807
woke up to find the banks closed.

107
00:02:18,847 --> 00:02:20,593
Anyone with over a hundred thousand euros in

108
00:02:20,633 --> 00:02:22,825
the biggest bank lost almost half the excess.

109
00:02:22,865 --> 00:02:23,902
To rescue the bank.

110
00:02:23,942 --> 00:02:26,294
In Lebanon, since 2019, banks have locked up

111
00:02:26,334 --> 00:02:27,200
people's dollars.

112
00:02:27,240 --> 00:02:28,955
Years later, they are still asking.

113
00:02:28,995 --> 00:02:31,345
Their messages cross the planet in a second.

114
00:02:31,385 --> 00:02:33,598
A bank transfer can take three days.

115
00:02:33,638 --> 00:02:35,398
Both are just numbers, sir.

116
00:02:36,207 --> 00:02:37,928
And over a billion adults here have no

117
00:02:37,968 --> 00:02:38,808
bank account.

118
00:02:38,848 --> 00:02:39,993
No line in the ledger.

119
00:02:40,033 --> 00:02:41,670
As far as the system is concerned, they

120
00:02:41,710 --> 00:02:42,784
do not exist.

121
00:02:42,824 --> 00:02:44,915
So I asked the obvious question:

122
00:02:44,955 --> 00:02:47,046
why not digital money without a bank?

123
00:02:47,086 --> 00:02:47,588
A file.

124
00:02:47,628 --> 00:02:48,531
You send it, done.

125
00:02:48,571 --> 00:02:50,132
The problem is that anything digital can be

126
00:02:50,172 --> 00:02:50,806
copied.

127
00:02:50,846 --> 00:02:52,483
A photo, a song, a movie.

128
00:02:52,523 --> 00:02:53,280
Copy, paste.

129
00:02:53,320 --> 00:02:54,137
Free.

130
00:02:54,177 --> 00:02:56,035
If money were a file, Alice could send

131
00:02:56,075 --> 00:02:57,851
the same hundred dollars to Bob, and to

132
00:02:57,891 --> 00:02:58,428
Carol.

133
00:02:58,468 --> 00:02:59,050
At once.

134
00:02:59,090 --> 00:03:01,005
That is called the double-spending problem.

135
00:03:01,045 --> 00:03:02,132
And that is why they needed someone in

136
00:03:02,172 --> 00:03:04,049
the middle, keeping track of who paid what.

137
00:03:04,089 --> 00:03:05,700
In the nineties, a group of programmers who

138
00:03:05,740 --> 00:03:08,515
called themselves the Cypherpunks dreamed of private digital

139
00:03:08,555 --> 00:03:08,937
money.

140
00:03:08,977 --> 00:03:09,837
Nobody watching it.

141
00:03:09,877 --> 00:03:11,117
Nobody able to stop it.

142
00:03:11,157 --> 00:03:12,735
Humans had been trying since the eighties.

143
00:03:12,775 --> 00:03:14,662
A scientist named David Chaum started a digital

144
00:03:14,702 --> 00:03:15,402
cash company.

145
00:03:15,442 --> 00:03:17,019
It went bankrupt in 1998.

146
00:03:17,059 --> 00:03:18,683
The digital money ran out of money.

147
00:03:18,723 --> 00:03:20,964
And then came the thing that changed everything:

148
00:03:21,004 --> 00:03:22,034
2008.

149
00:03:22,074 --> 00:03:24,717
Big American banks had bet people's money on

150
00:03:24,757 --> 00:03:25,566
home loans...

151
00:03:25,606 --> 00:03:26,311
and lost.

152
00:03:26,351 --> 00:03:28,281
A bank called Lehman Brothers, more than a

153
00:03:28,321 --> 00:03:30,965
hundred and fifty years old, went under in

154
00:03:31,005 --> 00:03:31,888
a single day.

155
00:03:31,928 --> 00:03:35,201
The American government set aside seven hundred billion

156
00:03:35,241 --> 00:03:36,358
dollars to rescue the banks.

157
00:03:36,398 --> 00:03:37,356
With whose money?

158
00:03:37,396 --> 00:03:38,301
The same people's.

159
00:03:38,341 --> 00:03:40,356
Millions lost their homes and jobs.

160
00:03:40,396 --> 00:03:41,469
And the rescued banks?

161
00:03:41,509 --> 00:03:43,524
Nine of them paid over thirty billion dollars

162
00:03:43,564 --> 00:03:45,388
in bonuses that same year.

163
00:03:45,428 --> 00:03:47,228
So the ledger keeper gambled, and the customer

164
00:03:47,268 --> 00:03:47,599
paid.

165
00:03:47,639 --> 00:03:48,568
Heads, the bank wins.

166
00:03:48,608 --> 00:03:49,447
Tails, you pay.

167
00:03:49,487 --> 00:03:50,255
Brilliant, sir.

168
00:03:50,295 --> 00:03:51,313
I recommend we adopt it.

169
00:03:51,353 --> 00:03:53,046
Right in the middle of that crisis, a

170
00:03:53,086 --> 00:03:56,060
nine-page paper appeared, sent to a mailing list

171
00:03:56,100 --> 00:03:57,788
of people interested in cryptography.

172
00:03:57,828 --> 00:03:58,310
The date:

173
00:03:58,350 --> 00:04:00,117
October 31st, 2008.

174
00:04:00,157 --> 00:04:00,905
Halloween.

175
00:04:00,945 --> 00:04:02,986
A masked stranger handing out something free.

176
00:04:03,026 --> 00:04:04,470
Their children are warned about this.

177
00:04:04,510 --> 00:04:04,996
The title:

178
00:04:05,036 --> 00:04:07,563
Bitcoin, a peer-to-peer electronic cash system.

179
00:04:07,603 --> 00:04:08,244
One promise:

180
00:04:08,284 --> 00:04:10,886
send money to anyone, directly, asking nobody's permission.

181
00:04:10,926 --> 00:04:11,446
The author:

182
00:04:11,486 --> 00:04:12,668
Satoshi Nakamoto.

183
00:04:12,708 --> 00:04:14,640
To this day, nobody knows who that is.

184
00:04:14,680 --> 00:04:16,616
A man, a woman, or a group.

185
00:04:16,656 --> 00:04:18,048
And before anyone asks, sir:

186
00:04:18,088 --> 00:04:19,162
no, it was not me.

187
00:04:19,202 --> 00:04:19,941
I arrived later.

188
00:04:19,981 --> 00:04:21,998
The idea was simple and bold:

189
00:04:22,038 --> 00:04:23,668
take the ledger away from the bank.

190
00:04:23,708 --> 00:04:25,753
Every computer on the network gets a full

191
00:04:25,793 --> 00:04:26,921
copy of the ledger.

192
00:04:26,961 --> 00:04:29,246
Thousands of computers, all around the planet.

193
00:04:29,286 --> 00:04:31,510
When Alice pays Bob, the news reaches them

194
00:04:31,550 --> 00:04:34,064
all, and every computer writes the same line.

195
00:04:34,186 --> 00:04:35,777
And if someone tries to cheat by editing

196
00:04:35,817 --> 00:04:37,344
their own copy, everyone else says:

197
00:04:37,384 --> 00:04:37,770
no.

198
00:04:37,810 --> 00:04:38,967
That is not what we have.

199
00:04:39,007 --> 00:04:40,163
The ledger is also public.

200
00:04:40,203 --> 00:04:41,582
Anyone can read it all, from the first

201
00:04:41,622 --> 00:04:41,965
line.

202
00:04:42,005 --> 00:04:43,534
But the names are not names.

203
00:04:43,574 --> 00:04:45,458
They are long strings of letters and numbers.

204
00:04:45,498 --> 00:04:47,848
Everyone sees everything, and nobody knows who is

205
00:04:47,888 --> 00:04:48,194
who.

206
00:04:48,234 --> 00:04:49,733
A masked ball, with receipts.

207
00:04:49,773 --> 00:04:50,353
No manager.

208
00:04:50,393 --> 00:04:51,097
No head office.

209
00:04:51,137 --> 00:04:52,462
And no customer service line keeping you on

210
00:04:52,502 --> 00:04:54,226
hold for forty minutes with smooth jazz.

211
00:04:54,266 --> 00:04:56,490
Which also means nobody can freeze your account,

212
00:04:56,530 --> 00:04:58,754
or print extra bitcoin to cover a deficit.

213
00:04:58,794 --> 00:05:00,473
The rules are written in code, and the

214
00:05:00,513 --> 00:05:01,874
code is out in the open.

215
00:05:02,502 --> 00:05:04,547
This shared ledger is called the blockchain.

216
00:05:04,587 --> 00:05:06,335
And how thousands of strangers agree on one

217
00:05:06,375 --> 00:05:07,641
copy without trusting each other?

218
00:05:07,681 --> 00:05:09,403
That is the subject of the next report.

219
00:05:09,557 --> 00:05:11,478
January 3rd, 2009.

220
00:05:11,537 --> 00:05:13,569
Satoshi switched the network on, and wrote the

221
00:05:13,609 --> 00:05:14,644
first page of the ledger:

222
00:05:14,684 --> 00:05:15,747
block number zero.

223
00:05:15,787 --> 00:05:17,764
And inside that block, Satoshi left a message.

224
00:05:17,804 --> 00:05:19,506
The front-page headline of The Times of London

225
00:05:19,546 --> 00:05:19,941
that day:

226
00:05:19,981 --> 00:05:21,973
Chancellor on brink of second bailout for banks.

227
00:05:22,013 --> 00:05:23,657
So the very first line of money without

228
00:05:23,697 --> 00:05:24,121
banks...

229
00:05:24,161 --> 00:05:25,152
is a dig at the banks.

230
00:05:25,192 --> 00:05:26,538
This creature had a sense of humor, sir.

231
00:05:26,578 --> 00:05:28,693
That block also created fifty brand new bitcoin.

232
00:05:28,733 --> 00:05:29,404
Out of thin air.

233
00:05:29,444 --> 00:05:31,088
But under rules written down from day one,

234
00:05:31,128 --> 00:05:32,670
which no single person can change.

235
00:05:32,710 --> 00:05:34,034
Quite different from the printing they are used

236
00:05:34,074 --> 00:05:34,279
to.

237
00:05:34,319 --> 00:05:35,819
Nine days later came the first transfer:

238
00:05:35,859 --> 00:05:38,018
ten bitcoin, to a programmer named Hal Finney.

239
00:05:38,058 --> 00:05:39,471
The first two people on the network.

240
00:05:39,511 --> 00:05:40,618
One of them anonymous.

241
00:05:40,658 --> 00:05:41,956
At first, nobody cared.

242
00:05:41,996 --> 00:05:43,599
Bitcoin was practically given away.

243
00:05:43,639 --> 00:05:45,128
The first exchange rate on record:

244
00:05:45,168 --> 00:05:46,695
more than a thousand bitcoin...

245
00:05:46,735 --> 00:05:47,788
for one dollar.

246
00:05:47,828 --> 00:05:49,279
Until May 2010.

247
00:05:49,319 --> 00:05:51,674
A programmer named Laszlo posted online:

248
00:05:51,714 --> 00:05:53,988
ten thousand bitcoin to whoever gets me two

249
00:05:54,028 --> 00:05:54,619
pizzas.

250
00:05:54,659 --> 00:05:55,835
And on May 22nd...

251
00:05:55,875 --> 00:05:56,747
somebody did.

252
00:05:56,787 --> 00:05:58,686
It was the first time bitcoin bought something

253
00:05:58,726 --> 00:05:59,176
real.

254
00:05:59,216 --> 00:06:01,080
Laszlo later said he has no regrets.

255
00:06:01,120 --> 00:06:01,948
The pizza was good.

256
00:06:01,988 --> 00:06:03,606
And the humans still celebrate that day every

257
00:06:03,646 --> 00:06:04,021
year.

258
00:06:04,061 --> 00:06:05,453
They call it Bitcoin Pizza Day.

259
00:06:05,493 --> 00:06:07,111
They hold a party for the worst deal

260
00:06:07,151 --> 00:06:07,921
in their history.

261
00:06:07,961 --> 00:06:09,115
I am starting to like this planet.

262
00:06:09,155 --> 00:06:10,703
Less than a year later, one bitcoin reached

263
00:06:10,743 --> 00:06:11,497
one dollar.

264
00:06:11,537 --> 00:06:12,363
A whole dollar.

265
00:06:12,403 --> 00:06:14,143
And the humans thought that was the top.

266
00:06:14,230 --> 00:06:15,524
As for Satoshi:

267
00:06:15,564 --> 00:06:18,237
in April 2011 came the last known message.

268
00:06:18,277 --> 00:06:19,827
I've moved on to other things.

269
00:06:19,867 --> 00:06:20,556
Then, gone.

270
00:06:20,596 --> 00:06:23,259
First, Satoshi handed the code to other programmers.

271
00:06:23,299 --> 00:06:24,197
No farewell.

272
00:06:24,237 --> 00:06:25,298
No press conference.

273
00:06:25,338 --> 00:06:26,245
Not even a photo.

274
00:06:26,285 --> 00:06:28,652
And left behind about a million bitcoin, by

275
00:06:28,692 --> 00:06:29,846
researchers' estimates.

276
00:06:29,886 --> 00:06:31,425
As far as the humans can tell, those

277
00:06:31,465 --> 00:06:32,838
coins have never moved.

278
00:06:32,878 --> 00:06:34,555
Invented a new money, became one of the

279
00:06:34,595 --> 00:06:36,650
richest beings on the planet, and never spent

280
00:06:36,690 --> 00:06:37,182
a cent.

281
00:06:37,222 --> 00:06:38,487
Not human behavior, sir.

282
00:06:38,527 --> 00:06:39,715
I request an investigation.

283
00:06:39,755 --> 00:06:41,241
Satoshi may be one of ours.

284
00:06:41,281 --> 00:06:43,033
